  },
  "body": "Serving a large immutable asset through `borrowBody()` was incorrectly limited by Baz\u2019s copied-body staging capacity. A 5 MiB image could not be borrowed with a small output arena, even though the HTTP engine already supports retained spans larger than its arena.\n\nThis change bounds borrowed bodies by `server.max_response_bytes` and keeps `response.body_bytes` for copied/generated bodies and streaming staging. It also avoids forming a staging slice for borrowed bodies during publication. App configures the total bound automatically; direct Response users get `initWithLimit`, while `init` preserves its previous conservative bound.\n\nThe lifetime contract remains retained request input or immutable server-lifetime assets. No dynamic heap/pool lease or zero-copy networking claim is introduced. Documentation now spells out copying and borrowing paths.\n\nLocal macOS validation passed: Debug/ReleaseSafe verification (68 root tests and one independent consumer test per mode), 14 App groups, 14 streaming groups, and all nine new borrowed-body wire groups. The new suite checks a 5 MiB binary asset through a 4 KiB arena, HEAD, partial progress, backpressure, cancellation, shutdown, and slot reuse. Linux/macOS/Windows CI includes it alongside existing App, example, and streaming gates; native CI results are pending.\n",
